% Value ownership: what is copied, what is aliased, and what a warm cache must
% never remember (spec/SPEC.md §3.4, §5.8, §5.9, §6.4; review 2026-09-29, family T03).
%
% Every expectation is derived from the spec, not from what a host printed. The
% rule, in one line: assignment copies, `,` copies what it collects, the §7.3
% aggregates copy what they collect, nothing else copies -- and a list a function
% returns is always a NEW container, whatever it does about its elements.
%
% Observing a copy needs a mutation between the moment a value is yielded and the
% moment it is read, which is what the index-expression idiom `X[(mutate; key)]`
% gives (§3.4): the base is yielded first, the index expression mutates the
% source, and the pending read then finds whatever the base still shares.
%
% Cases that a spec decision has not yet settled (does LIST/RECORD copy its
% arguments; do TAKE/DROP/DISTINCT/TOP/LINK copy their elements) are deliberately
% absent -- see the family's open questions in docs/interim/2026-09-29/worklist.

### name: alias.comma-copies-a-child
--- note
A child contributed by `,` is copied (§3.4, §5.9): the mutation made by the second
operand must not show through the first. PHP once kept the child, and answered 99
where every other host answered 1 (PHP-C2).
--- source
A = RECORD("a", RECORD("b",1)); (A, A["a"]["b"] = 99)[1]["b"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.comma-copies-a-value
--- note
A value with a scalar of its own contributes itself, and `,` copies it too. A is
text "x" with a child "k"; the second operand rewrites the child's child.
--- source
A = "x"; A["k"] = RECORD("b",1); (A, A["k"]["b"] = 99)[1]["k"]["b"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.comma-copies-flattened-children
--- note
Both operands are lists whose children are contributed one by one; every child is
copied, so the list built from `X, X` is independent of X.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); (X, X)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.comma-is-a-new-container
--- note
CONTROL. The list `,` builds is a new container: replacing one of X's children
afterwards does not change it.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); (A, 4)[(A[1] = 9; "1")]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.map
--- note
`MAP(X, _)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); MAP(X, _)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.filter
--- note
`FILTER(X, TRUE)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); FILTER(X, TRUE)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.sort
--- note
`SORT(X)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); SORT(X)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.sort-body
--- note
`SORT(X, 1)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); SORT(X, 1)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.sort-desc
--- note
`SORT_DESC(X, 1)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); SORT_DESC(X, 1)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.sort-by
--- note
`SORT_BY(X, 1)` collects X's element into its result, and §3.4 has the aggregates copy what
they collect: the element mutated through X while the result is still pending is
not the element the result holds. The hosts disagreed (contributing.md's claim
that the copy cannot be observed is false, GO-C27, CPP-C42, LISP-C35).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); SORT_BY(X, 1)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.bucket-two-argument
--- note
BUCKET's two-argument spelling groups the elements into lists inside a record; the
members it collects are copies (§3.4).
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); BUCKET(X, 1)["1"][(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.bucket-three-argument
--- note
The three-argument spelling collects one projection per group; here the projection
is the member list, and what it collected is copied.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); BUCKET(X, 1, _)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)][1]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.bucket-by-field
--- note
The same with a key read from the element, the shape a report actually writes.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("k",1)); BUCKET(X, _["k"], _)[(X[1]["k"] = 9; 1)][1]["k"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: alias.aggregate-copies.body-mutates-source-during-iteration
--- note
The body assigns into the source while MAP is collecting: the first result is
what the body returned for element 1, and element 2's mutation is not visible in
it (both hosts and spec agree the result is a snapshot of what was collected).
--- source
R = LIST(RECORD("id",1), RECORD("id",2)); MAP(R, (R[2]["id"] = 99; _))[(R[1]["id"] = 7; 1)]["id"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: struct.take.result-is-a-new-list
--- note
A list a function returns is a new container: replacing A's first child while
`TAKE(A,2)` is still pending must not change what the result holds. Go's TAKE and DROP
returned a slice of A's backing array and showed the 9 (GO-C7).
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); TAKE(A,2)[(A[1] = 9; "1")]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: struct.drop.result-is-a-new-list
--- note
A list a function returns is a new container: replacing A's first child while
`DROP(A,0)` is still pending must not change what the result holds. Go's TAKE and DROP
returned a slice of A's backing array and showed the 9 (GO-C7).
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); DROP(A,0)[(A[1] = 9; "1")]
--- expect
num 1
===

