### name: struct.bucket.result-is-a-new-list
--- note
A list a function returns is a new container: replacing A's first child while
`BUCKET(A, 1)["1"]` is still pending must not change what the result holds. Go's TAKE and DROP
returned a slice of A's backing array and showed the 9 (GO-C7).
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); BUCKET(A, 1)["1"][(A[1] = 9; "1")]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: struct.take.detached-from-source
--- note
GO-C7's own repro: TAKE's result is dumped after the source was assigned to.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); LIST(TAKE(A,2), A[1] = 9)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}, "2"=t"9"}
===

### name: struct.drop.detached-from-source
--- note
The DROP twin of struct.take.detached-from-source.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); LIST(DROP(A,1), A[2] = 9)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"2", "2"=t"3"}, "2"=t"9"}
===

### name: struct.drop.detached-from-source-pending-read
--- note
The pending-read form: DROP's result is read after A's second child was replaced.
--- source
A = LIST(1,2,3); DROP(A,1)[(A[2] = 9; "1")]
--- expect
num 2
===

### name: cache.decimal.reassigned-variable
--- note
A variable that held numeric-looking text and was used as a number is reassigned
non-numeric text: nothing about the old text may be remembered.
--- source
A = "12"; B = ISNUM(A); N = A + 1; A = "abc"; ISNUM(A)
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: cache.decimal.reassigned-child
--- note
The same through an index assignment, where the child is replaced in a shared
container after arithmetic warmed its decimal.
--- source
R = RECORD("a","12"); N = R["a"] + 1; R["a"] = "abc"; ISNUM(R["a"])
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: cache.decimal.reassigned-child-arithmetic
--- note
And arithmetic on the replaced child fails at the use, not with the old number.
--- source
R = RECORD("a","12"); N = R["a"] + 1; R["a"] = "abc"; R["a"] + 1
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:56
===

### name: cache.decimal.scalar-with-children-reassigned
--- note
A scalar that gained a child and was used as a number, then replaced.
--- source
A = "5"; A["k"] = 1; X = A + 1; A = "x"; ISNUM(A)
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: cache.identity.distinct-after-mutation
--- note
DISTINCT warmed the record's identity hash; mutating the record must be seen by
the next DISTINCT.
--- source
R = RECORD("a",1,"b",2); K = COUNT(DISTINCT(LIST(R, RECORD("a",1,"b",2)))); R["a"] = 5; K & "/" & COUNT(DISTINCT(LIST(R, RECORD("a",1,"b",2))))
--- expect
text "1/2"
===

### name: cache.identity.eql-after-mutation
--- note
EQL after one side changed.
--- source
R = RECORD("a",1); S = RECORD("a",1); X = IF(R EQL S, "eq", "ne"); S["a"] = 2; X & "/" & IF(R EQL S, "eq", "ne")
--- expect
text "eq/ne"
===

### name: cache.identity.bucket-after-added-key
--- note
BUCKET groups by identity; a key added to one member afterwards splits the group.
--- source
R = RECORD("a",1); S = RECORD("a",1); X = COUNT(BUCKET(LIST(R,S), _, COUNT(_))); S["b"] = 2; X & "/" & COUNT(BUCKET(LIST(R,S), _, COUNT(_)))
--- expect
text "1/2"
===

### name: cache.identity.shared-element-after-mutation
--- note
The same element twice: identical until it is mutated, and then it is still one
element (the second list holds a different record, so the count is 2).
--- source
R = RECORD("k",1); D1 = COUNT(DISTINCT(LIST(R, R))); R["k"] = 2; D1 & "/" & COUNT(DISTINCT(LIST(R, RECORD("k", 1))))
--- expect
text "1/2"
===

### name: cache.order.reassigning-a-key-keeps-its-position
--- note
Iteration order is insertion order, and overwriting a key does not move it.
--- source
R = RECORD("b",1,"a",2); R["b"] = 3; JOIN(INDEXES(R), ",") & "/" & R["b"]
--- expect
text "b,a/3"
===

### name: cache.sort.after-element-replaced
--- note
SORT warmed each element's numeric look; the replaced element sorts as text.
--- source
L = LIST("10", 9); X = SORT(L); L[1] = "abc"; JOIN(SORT(L), ",")
--- expect
text "9,abc"
===

### name: alias.assignment-copies-nested-children
--- note
CONTROL. Assignment deep-copies (§5.8): S does not share R's nested child.
--- source
R = RECORD("a", RECORD("b", 1)); S = R; S["a"]["b"] = 2; R["a"]["b"]
--- expect
num 1
===

### name: record.shape.width-14-assigned-key-does-not-leak
--- note
A 14-field record gains a key; a record built afterwards from the same 14 keys
has exactly those keys and not the added one. Lisp's shape table was corrupted
from 16 fields (LISP-C2). Cases run in one process, so a shape poisoned here is
seen by the cases below.
--- source
R = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); R["Z"] = 5; S = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); HAS(S, "Z")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===

### name: record.shape.width-14-count-and-read
--- note
The same records read back: count 14, the last field present, and the key the
first record gained absent (E_NO_KEY at the index).
--- source
R = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); R["Z"] = 5; S = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); COUNT(S) & "/" & S["K14"]
--- expect
text "14/14"
===

### name: record.shape.width-14-missing-key-is-e-no-key
--- note
Reading the added key from a fresh record is the ordinary E_NO_KEY, never a host
array-index failure.
--- source
R = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); R["Z"] = 5; S = RECORD("K1", 1, "K2", 2, "K3", 3, "K4", 4, "K5", 5, "K6", 6, "K7", 7, "K8", 8, "K9", 9, "K10", 10, "K11", 11, "K12", 12, "K13", 13, "K14", 14); S["Z"]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:310
===

